  {"question": "According to arXiv 2401.10896, which AI governance frameworks address both stakeholder involvement and specific AI development stages, and what is noted about their completeness?", "answer": "Only one framework, from study N29, addresses both stakeholder involvement and specific AI development stages, and it is the only one among five frameworks to answer all 3W1H questions.", "evidence": [{"page": 6, "quote": "only one article [N29] out of five studies on AI governance frameworks has added answers to all questions of Who, What, When, and How."}, {"page": 5, "quote": "only 4 [N15] [N29] [E45] [E47] had answers to all questions in 3W1H, which means that they are clear on who should be responsible for regulating the AI through these solutions and at what development stage."}]},

  {"question": "In arXiv 2401.10896, which AI governance challenges are most discussed, and what is said about human involvement in current approaches?", "answer": "Ethical challenges like fairness, transparency, privacy, and trust are most discussed; lack of human involvement is noted as making AI less human-centric.", "evidence": [{"page": 7, "quote": "Ethical challenges, including fairness, transparency, privacy, and trust, are the most discussed in AI governance according to the literature [N4], [N9], [E40], [E49], and [N60]. Lack of human involvement in current AI governance approaches makes the AI system less human-centric [N4] [E40] [E41]."}]},

  {"question": "What are the four pillars of the AI ecosystem described in arXiv 2401.10896, and how is the 'humans' pillar uniquely linked to governance roles?", "answer": "The four pillars are humans, data, processes, and systems. The 'humans' pillar uniquely links operational AI practitioners to oversight roles, focusing on 'who is governing whom?'.", "evidence": [{"page": 3, "quote": "The selection of these four pillars is grounded in a thorough review of the existing literature and a consideration of the fundamental elements that constitute the AI ecosystem [6]. Aligning with Zowghi and da Rimini's study [6], each pillar is further explained below. · Humans: Acknowledging the centrality of human involvement in AI development and deployment, this pillar encompasses stakeholders (operational AI practitioners) involved in the day-to-day technical implementation and operational aspects of AI projects, such as AI developers, AI designers, etc. From the previous question, 'Who is governing?', which includes key AI oversight roles, this pillar is linked with a focus on observing 'Who is governing whom?', which"}]},

  {"question": "According to arXiv 2401.10896, what is the 3W1H approach and how are its elements distributed across AI governance levels?", "answer": "The 3W1H approach asks Who, What, When, and How regarding AI governance, and its key elements are categorized under team, organisation, industry, national, and international levels.", "evidence": [{"page": 2, "quote": "3W1H approach is presented in this paper, and the categorisation of key elements from 3W1H questions is covered under different layers of governance from the study by Lu et al. [1]: team-level, organisation-level, industry-level, national-level, and internationallevel, which are explained later in this paper."}]},

  {"question": "In arXiv 2401.10896, which AI application domains are most frequently targeted by governance solutions, and how many studies did not specify a domain?", "answer": "Healthcare is most frequently targeted (10 studies), followed by robotics (3 studies); 25 studies did not specify an application domain.", "evidence": [{"page": 7, "quote": "It has been analysed that the most common AI application domains targeted in the current AI governance solutions are healthcare and robotics. The results (Fig. 9) show that 10 of the research studies on frameworks, models, tools, and policies has targeted healthcare as the application domain. Robotics is targeted in three studies. Other domains include banking, media and communication, academia, and so on. There are 25 studies that have not specified the application domains."}]},

  {"question": "Which study in arXiv 2401.10896 is noted for proposing a governance model for AI in healthcare, and what is its main focus?", "answer": "Study [3] is noted for proposing a governance model for AI in healthcare, focusing on the application of AI in health care sectors.", "evidence": [{"page": 1, "quote": "a governance model for the AI application in healthcare sectors [3]."}]}
]}
